n E1, E2 and E3 against White's F-file chain."}, {"turn": 25, "text": "GPT-6.1 Sol's J6 lost 8.5 points and turned a +4.9 lead into -3.5. The engine preferred B3, which would have kept the left side from filling up with White stones."}, {"turn": 26, "text": "The 20k answered with B3 and lost 8.7 points, giving the lead right back (-3.5 → +5.2). Two big errors in a row left the game where it had been."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "C2 was the biggest swing of the game, a 28.5-point loss that dropped Black from +5.6 to -22.8. The engine's choice was A2, and the stone on C2 left Black's corner cutting stones short of liberties."}, {"turn": 30, "text": "The 20k found C1, which cost 27.3 points and put the lead back at +4.5 for Black. Both sides had misjudged the corner badly, and it was still unsettled afterward."}, {"turn": 37, "text": "E4 lost 7.3 points and took Black from +4.0 to -3.3, with B6 the engine's choice. It was the last big error, and the corner trades that followed (B8, C8, B9 against B7, B6, A8, A7) ended with the lead on White's side at -2.3 at the end."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "game-031-inspection.js (parseBoard, neighbors, inspect, save, play) only parses returned boards, computes group liberties and empty regions, and logs responses, with each move passed in by hand; the later replay of recorded moves, area count, SGF and review writing all work on the played history, and nothing loops over candidate moves or looks ahead.", "stop": false}}